      "clips": [
        {
          "clipName": "clip0",
          "audioClip": "heart_intro_overview",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "HeartModel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "HeartTitleLabel",
              "enabled": true
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "The heart model appears on the table with a slow rotation and a title label above it, giving students an initial orientation to the 3D specimen.",
          "narration": "Welcome to the Human Heart lab. In front of you is a three-dimensional model of the adult human heart, oriented in the anatomical position. The heart is a muscular pump about the size of your fist, located in the mediastinum of the thoracic cavity. It beats approximately 100,000 times per day, driving blood through roughly 60,000 miles of blood vessels. Over the next few scenes, we will explore its internal architecture and trace the journey blood takes through it."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip1",
          "audioClip": "heart_four_chambers",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "RightAtriumLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "LeftAtriumLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "RightVentricleLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "LeftVentricleLabel",
              "enabled": true
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "Labels for all four chambers appear next to the heart model simultaneously, color-coded blue for the right (deoxygenated) side and red for the left (oxygenated) side to build a durable visual association.",
          "narration": "The heart is divided into four hollow chambers. The two upper chambers are called atria \u2014 the right atrium and the left atrium. They receive incoming blood. The two lower chambers are the ventricles \u2014 the right ventricle and the left ventricle. They are the powerful pumping chambers that eject blood out of the heart. A thick muscular wall called the interventricular septum separates the left and right sides, ensuring oxygenated and deoxygenated blood never mix."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip2",
          "audioClip": "heart_four_valves",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "TricuspidValveMarker",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "TricuspidLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "PulmonaryValveMarker",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "PulmonaryValveLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "MitralValveMarker",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "MitralValveLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "AorticValveMarker",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "AorticValveLabel",
              "enabled": true
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "Glowing valve markers and their text labels appear at the correct anatomical locations on the heart model, each in a distinct color so students can identify all four valves simultaneously.",
          "narration": "Separating each chamber pair is a one-way valve that prevents backflow of blood. Between the right atrium and right ventricle sits the tricuspid valve, named for its three leaflets or cusps. Between the left atrium and left ventricle is the mitral valve \u2014 also called the bicuspid valve \u2014 which has two cusps. As blood leaves the right ventricle toward the lungs it passes through the pulmonary semilunar valve. And as blood leaves the left ventricle to enter the aorta it passes through the aortic semilunar valve. All four valves open and close passively in response to pressure differences during each heartbeat."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip3",
          "audioClip": "heart_major_vessels",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "SuperiorVenaCavaModel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "SVCLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "InferiorVenaCavaModel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "IVCLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "AortaModel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "AortaLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "PulmonaryArteryModel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "PulmonaryArteryLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "PulmonaryVeinModel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "PulmonaryVeinLabel",
              "enabled": true
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "All major vessel models and their labels are revealed on the heart model: blue vessels for veins and deoxygenated pathways, red for arteries carrying oxygenated blood, reinforcing colour conventions used throughout medicine.",
          "narration": "Several large vessels attach to the heart. Entering the right atrium from above is the superior vena cava, carrying deoxygenated blood from the head, neck, and arms. Entering from below is the inferior vena cava, draining the abdomen and lower limbs. The pulmonary trunk leaves the right ventricle and splits into the left and right pulmonary arteries, which carry deoxygenated blood to the lungs. Four pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood from the lungs into the left atrium. Finally, the aorta \u2014 the body's largest artery \u2014 arises from the left ventricle and distributes oxygenated blood to the entire systemic circulation."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip4",
          "audioClip": "heart_ventricular_wall_thickness",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "VentricleCompareLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "HeartModel",
              "eulerAngles": [
                0,
                30,
                0
              ]
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "A callout label appears beneath the heart model drawing attention to the wall thickness difference between the ventricles, anchoring the functional explanation to a visible anatomical feature.",
          "narration": "Notice that the left ventricle has a significantly thicker muscular wall than the right ventricle \u2014 approximately three times thicker on average. This makes functional sense: the right ventricle only needs to pump blood the short distance to the lungs, a low-resistance circuit. The left ventricle, however, must generate enough pressure to push blood through the entire systemic circulation \u2014 from the aorta all the way to the tips of your fingers and toes and back. Greater workload demands greater muscle mass. In patients with chronic hypertension, the left ventricular wall can hypertrophy even further as the heart works against persistently elevated resistance."
        }
      ]
