          ],
          "changeMeaning": "The cross-section heart model and the dual-loop title label appear. Lung models appear on either side, establishing the spatial relationship between the heart and lungs that anchors the entire circulation pathway.",
          "narration": "Now that you know the structures, let's watch blood move through the heart. The heart drives two distinct circuits simultaneously \u2014 the pulmonary circuit, which carries blood to and from the lungs, and the systemic circuit, which supplies the rest of the body. These two loops run in series: blood must complete one before entering the other. We will follow a single red blood cell beginning its journey as deoxygenated blood returning from the body."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip6",
          "audioClip": "flow_right_heart_pulmonary",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "DeoxyBloodParticle",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "RightAtriumFlowLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "TricuspidFlowLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "PulmonaryFlowLabel",
              "enabled": true
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "The blue deoxygenated blood particle activates and begins animating along its waypoint path through the right heart. Step-by-step flow labels appear for the right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, and pulmonary artery, guiding the student's eye through the right-heart sequence.",
          "narration": "Deoxygenated blood, shown here in blue, drains from the body into the right atrium via the superior and inferior vena cavae. When the right atrium contracts, pressure rises and the tricuspid valve swings open, allowing blood to pour into the right ventricle below. Once the right ventricle is full, it contracts powerfully \u2014 the tricuspid valve snaps shut to prevent backflow while the pulmonary semilunar valve opens. Blood is now propelled into the pulmonary trunk, which branches into the left and right pulmonary arteries and heads to the lungs. Notice that we call these pulmonary arteries even though they carry deoxygenated blood \u2014 arteries are simply defined as vessels carrying blood away from the heart."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip7",
          "audioClip": "flow_gas_exchange_return",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "OxyBloodParticle",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "LeftAtriumFlowLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "LungModelLeft",
              "scale": [
                0.2,
                0.25,
                0.16
              ]
            },
            {
              "target": "LungModelRight",
              "scale": [
                0.2,
                0.25,
                0.16
              ]
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "The red oxygenated blood particle activates at the lungs. The left-atrium flow label appears. The lung models pulse slightly in scale to suggest active gas exchange, drawing attention to the site of oxygen loading.",
          "narration": "Inside the tiny capillaries surrounding the alveoli of the lungs, carbon dioxide diffuses out of the blood and oxygen diffuses in. Our once-blue deoxygenated blood is now bright red with oxygen. The oxygenated blood collects in the pulmonary venules and drains into four pulmonary veins \u2014 two from each lung \u2014 which deposit it into the left atrium. Again, note the naming convention: these are called pulmonary veins because they carry blood toward the heart, even though the blood they carry is oxygen-rich. The left atrium is a relatively thin-walled, low-pressure chamber whose primary job is to collect and briefly store this incoming oxygenated blood."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip8",
          "audioClip": "flow_left_heart_systemic",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "MitralFlowLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "AorticFlowLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "PulmonaryCircuitLabel",
              "enabled": false
            },
            {
              "target": "SystemicCircuitLabel",
              "enabled": true
            },
            {
              "target": "HeartCrossSection",
              "components": [
                {
                  "componentType": "simpleRotation",
                  "timeRate": 1.5,
                  "rotationTime": 20
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "The mitral and aortic flow labels appear, completing the left-heart pathway. The systemic circuit label replaces the pulmonary circuit label to signify the transition to the second loop. The heart model slightly increases its rotation speed to evoke the pumping action of ventricular contraction.",
          "narration": "When the left atrium contracts, the mitral \u2014 or bicuspid \u2014 valve opens, and oxygenated blood flows down into the left ventricle. The left ventricle is the powerhouse of the heart. When it contracts, the mitral valve closes and the aortic semilunar valve opens, launching blood into the aorta at a pressure of roughly 120 millimetres of mercury during systole. From there, blood branches into every organ and tissue of the body \u2014 delivering oxygen, nutrients, and hormones \u2014 before returning, now deoxygenated, through the venous system back to the right atrium to begin the cycle again."
        },
        {
          "clipName": "clip9",
          "audioClip": "flow_valve_backflow_summary",
          "autoAdvance": false,
          "changes": [
            {
              "target": "StepNarratorLabel",
              "enabled": true,
              "components": [
                {
                  "componentType": "textMeshPro",
                  "text": "Valve Summary:\nTricuspid (RA\u2192RV)  |  Pulmonary (RV\u2192Pulm. A.)\nMitral (LA\u2192LV)  |  Aortic (LV\u2192Aorta)\nAll valves prevent backflow via passive pressure gradients.",
                  "color": [
                    255,
                    255,
                    180,
                    255
                  ],
                  "fontSize": 22,
                  "wrapText": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "target": "SystemicCircuitLabel",
              "enabled": false
            }
          ],
          "changeMeaning": "A summary status label appears beneath the heart recapping all four valves and their positions in the flow path. Both blood particles continue their animated loops, reinforcing the continuous, cyclical nature of cardiac output as the narrator delivers the closing summary.",
          "narration": "Let's take a moment to appreciate the valves. Every valve in the heart is passive \u2014 it requires no muscular control. Each opens simply because pressure upstream exceeds pressure downstream, and closes when that gradient reverses. The two atrioventricular valves \u2014 the tricuspid on the right and the mitral on the left \u2014 are anchored by tough fibrous chords called chordae tendineae attached to papillary muscles on the ventricular walls. These cords prevent the valve leaflets from prolapsing backward into the atria during the high-pressure ventricular contraction. The two semilunar valves \u2014 the pulmonary and the aortic \u2014 have crescent-shaped cusps that fill with blood and snap shut when arterial pressure exceeds ventricular pressure. This elegant mechanical system ensures unidirectional flow throughout the cardiac cycle."
        }
